# acestream-suite's guide, refreshed from its XMLTV sources.
#
# Installed through scripts/crons-install.sh (this file is in its JOBS list, and the host crontab is
# generated from it; /etc/cron.d is never edited by hand on this host).
#
# Why a host cron and not a timer inside the container: the image is the app's, nothing in it runs
# on a schedule (the entrypoint migrates and serves), and a second entrypoint that slept in a loop
# would be one more thing to keep in step with the app. The refresh is a command, and the host
# already runs a cron for the playlist acestream-suite publishes.
#
# The interval IS this schedule: 04:07 and 16:07 are twelve hours apart, and nothing else declares
# it (the compose used to carry a variable nobody read; it is gone). Changing how often the guide
# refreshes means changing this line, in one place. 12 h because the
# providers publish a day or two of listings and re-cut a few times a day; more often is bandwidth
# for nothing. Measured on 2026-10-14: a full refresh of both sources is ~1 min and ~25,000
# programmes in the window, and it prunes what has gone past as it goes.
#
# 04:07 and 16:07 and not `*/12`, which would fire on the hour and meet the nightly backups. The
# offset is deliberate, in the spirit of the playlist job's 5,20,35,50.
#
# flock because the command can also be run by hand while this fires — verified that way when it
# was installed — and two refreshes at once would fight over the same SQLite file.
#
# The command reports per source and exits non-zero only when no source answered at all, so a moved
# community feed leaves a line in this log and keeps the previous guide. Nothing rotates the log:
# one run is a handful of lines, and a failed one is worth finding.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin
7 4,16 * * * root [ -r /etc/acestream-suite.env ] && . /etc/acestream-suite.env; /usr/bin/flock -n /var/lock/acestream-suite-guide-refresh.lock docker exec acestream-suite python3 -m app.cli epg-refresh >>/var/log/acestream-suite-guide-refresh.log 2>&1
